### 6. Competitive Landscape

Note: Web search was not performed. The following is based on training knowledge of the content landscape as of early 2025.

**What exists:**
- McKinsey, Gartner, and Deloitte reports on AI adoption (paywalled, enterprise-focused, heavy on framework, light on practitioner specifics)
- "Top 10 AI adoption best practices" listicles (tool-centric, generic, no data on failure rates)
- Vendor-specific implementation guides (Microsoft Copilot, Google Workspace AI) — positioned to sell, not to advise
- Change management pieces on AI that focus on reskilling and training budgets — rarely name the identity-shift problem directly

**What existing content misses:**
- The practitioner's honest failure rate data (McKinsey 80% net-negative, MIT 95% pilot trap) cited together as a unified indictment of tool-first adoption
- The "departmental maturity not company maturity" reframe — most content treats AI strategy as monolithic
- The saboteur dynamic — forced adoption creating active resistance, even unintentionally
- The 130x model cost differential as a risk to an otherwise strong ROI
- The hybrid intelligence failure rate data as a direct rebuttal to replacement fears

**How this article differs:**
Practitioner voice, internal examples (including an embarrassing one), named failure modes, and cited statistics rather than framework abstraction. It takes a side rather than hedging. The audience is a CTO or VP who has already been told to "do AI" and needs to know why their current approach is probably wrong.

**SEO opportunities:**
- "Why AI projects fail to scale" — high practitioner search intent, existing content is abstract
- "AI strategy for enterprise organizations" — crowded but most results are vendor-led
- "How to build an AI adoption strategy" — question-based, featured snippet opportunity

**Primary search intent:** Practitioner seeking a reality-check on why their AI initiative isn't delivering, or a leader asked to build an AI strategy who wants to do it right the first time.

